{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested for task/ui-thread-event-railway at exactly 04fa4a38433ab5ee4ee9cf50f9f7ae0267867d45.\n\nThe lane semantics, the first-basis/citation split, and the generated bundle are all correct and well pinned. Two things stop me approving: the railway is unusable on 14 per cent of this workroom's own threads because lane count is unbounded, and four of the five conditions in the task request are not pinned by any test.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED, AND HOW\n\nCheckout. /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/ui-thread-event-railway is clean (empty git status --porcelain), HEAD is exactly 04fa4a38433ab5ee4ee9cf50f9f7ae0267867d45, current main (0a66e85) is its direct parent and an ancestor, git diff --check is clean, and the single commit carries Rests-On: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ea50a7f70c050dc47977baf38a3d746d19604b0f. I did not modify that worktree; all experiments ran in two detached scratch worktrees at the same head, since removed.\n\nGates, in a scratch worktree at this head. make vet clean. make test all thirteen packages ok. cd spike \u0026\u0026 go test -race ./... all thirteen packages ok (kernel 260s). cd ui \u0026\u0026 npm ci \u0026\u0026 npm test 36 of 36 pass. npm run build (tsc -b \u0026\u0026 vite build) passes. This matches the report's stated evidence.\n\nGenerated UI assets. Rebuilt from source in a clean scratch worktree at this head: after npm ci \u0026\u0026 npm run build, git status --porcelain is empty. The committed spike/internal/service/uidist/ is byte-identical to the build output at this head, including the index-BWio7wf2.css and index-DLm-kCZS.js content hashes.\n\nOrdering invariant, against real data. I walked all 714 durable threads in this workroom's live projection through the shipped modules. In every thread, each node's first basis is laid out before it: zero out-of-order parents and zero forward citations. So the \"no parent\" stub edge only ever fires for the thread root, as intended, and lane assignment never reads a node it has not yet placed.\n\nFINDING 1 — unbounded lanes make the railway unusable on 100 of 714 real threads\n\nui/src/lib/threadRailway.ts, layoutThreadRailway. Every non-first child takes lane = nextLane++, and no lane is ever released. Lane count therefore grows with the total number of branch points in a thread, not with the number of conversation branches open at any given row.\n\nMeasured, not reasoned. I loaded the shipped src/lib/threads.ts, src/lib/threadRailway.ts and src/components/ThreadRailway.tsx through Vite SSR and ran them over this repository's live /v0/status (1171 events). Of the 714 durable threads that have replies, 100 lay out a rail wider than the 360px content box of the 24rem pane, and 146 leave under 120px for the event label. The widest, ticket #19 (\"Accepted dogfood checkpoint\" — an ordinary stream row, one click to open, one more to the Railway tab), lays out 928 rows across 367 lanes and renders \u003csvg width=\"8098\" height=\"63104\"\u003e with the first row's inline style padding-left: 8102px. The row's label button is min-w-0 flex-1 inside that padding, so its content box collapses to zero and no event text survives.\n\nThis is the task condition \"remains usable in the narrow panel and on phones\". The report offers the \"18rem compact rail floor\" as the answer, but min-w-[18rem] on ThreadRailway.tsx:72 is a floor, not a cap; it does nothing above about fifteen lanes, where graphWidth alone exceeds the pane.\n\nThe codebase already has the counter-pattern. ui/src/components/Railway.tsx (lines 23-51) recycles lanes: a lane holds the hash it expects next, is cleared when that hash lands, and lanes.indexOf(null) reclaims it. Simulating that policy on the same 714 threads takes the worst thread from 367 lanes to 100 and the over-wide threads from 100 to 19 — so recycling is necessary but not sufficient here, because these old roots genuinely have around a hundred long-lived direct children. Some cap or fold looks needed as well. That is the implementer's call; I am reporting the failure, not prescribing the fix.\n\nOne honesty note: browser automation was unavailable to me too, so the geometry above is read off rendered markup, not a screenshot. Whether the collapsed label ends up clipped or merely unreachable by scrolling, it is not readable either way.\n\nFINDING 2 — four of the five stated conditions are not pinned by any test\n\nThe task request required \"focused UI tests\" for its conditions. I mutated the head in a scratch worktree and ran the suite; each of these left all 36 tests passing, so no test can fail if the behaviour is lost:\n\n- Reversing the railway to newest-first (ThreadRailway.tsx:51, the ordered memo) — the \"preserves root-first event order\" condition. Nothing observes order.\n- Deleting every semantic-status marker from the railway rows — ratified badge, stale flag, retired strike-through, ineffective CircleSlash, withdrawn Undo2 (ThreadRailway.tsx:103-107) — the \"semantic status\" condition.\n- Replacing onJumpTo(node.event) with a no-op on every row (ThreadRailway.tsx:87) — the \"retains event navigation\" condition.\n- Dropping the view === \"railway\" guard so the railway renders under both tabs (ThreadPane.tsx:155) — the tab switch itself is untested; only the tab markup is.\n- Removing min-w-[18rem] and overflow-auto (ThreadRailway.tsx:71-72) — the narrow-panel condition, which is also Finding 1.\n- Deleting setView(\"thread\") from the retarget effect (ThreadPane.tsx:84), so the pane keeps the previous thread's railway when it retargets.\n\nFor contrast, these mutations did fail a test, so those parts are genuinely pinned and I am satisfied with them: first-child lane continuation (lane = siblings === 0 ? parent.lane : nextLane++); citations: bases.slice(1); bases[0] as the conversational parent rather than the last basis; the dashed citation stroke; inclusion of the root row; and the tablist and tab roles.\n\nFINDING 3 — minor: \"outside thread\" does not mean outside the thread\n\nThreadRailway.tsx:65-68 chooses between \"#N\" and \"outside thread\" on whether tickets has the event, but ticketsOf (ui/src/lib/store.ts:152) tickets every event in the projection. A base that really is outside the thread but inside the projection therefore renders as \"reply to #N\" or \"cites #N\" with no edge drawn and no sign that it is off-thread — which is the case for every railway root row. The literal words \"outside thread\" appear only when a bounded status did not carry the event at all. Either reword the fallback or test membership against the laid-out node set, which byNode already holds. The report's claim that the railway \"names off-thread bases\" is true in the weak sense that it prints a ticket; it does not mark them as off-thread.\n\nOBSERVATIONS I DELIBERATELY DID NOT RAISE AS FINDINGS\n\n- Tabs. role=\"tablist\" with aria-label, role=\"tab\" and aria-selected are present, but there is no id or aria-controls, no roving tabindex, and no Left/Right arrow handling; the tabpanel is named by aria-label rather than aria-labelledby and is not focusable although it is the scroll container. This is short of the ARIA authoring-practices tabs pattern, but both tabs are real buttons reachable by Tab and activated by Enter or Space, and everything is named, so the surface is operable. Worth a follow-up, not a blocker.\n- The rail's horizontal scrollbar sits at the bottom of an element as tall as the entire thread, so it is effectively out of reach on long threads. Subsumed by Finding 1.\n- The SVG is aria-hidden and pointer-events-none, with the structure carried textually per row (\"reply to #N\", \"cites #N\"). I checked this and think it is the right call.\n- Fixed 68px rows with truncate clip long statement text. Consistent with the existing commit railway; not raised.\n- Row count is not a railway regression: the Thread view already renders all 928 descendants for that thread. What the railway adds is the horizontal blowout.\n\nWHAT I COULD NOT VERIFY\n\nLive browser behaviour — real focus order, actual painted overflow, and phone rendering — was not available in this session, same as for the implementer. Everything above comes from the shipped modules rendered server-side against the workroom's own projection, plus the mutation experiments.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8cbb25f8f0679db2419fa0944e9f722c5b8a2a7a","head":"04fa4a38433ab5ee4ee9cf50f9f7ae0267867d45","verdict":"changes-requested"}}